# The Help page's topics: titles, search words, paragraphs, tips, steps and picture captions. Part of Settings' own words (see settings-help.ftl).


## Topic: AI programs (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "AI programs".
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-title = AI programs
# Words people might search for the Help topic "AI programs" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-keywords = ai claude codex cursor gemini aider coding agent fence private files share folder ssh keys sudo
# Help topic "AI programs": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-p1 = This keeps AI programs away from your personal files in everyday use, for example if one is tricked into collecting your bank statements and sending them out. It is a limited protection, not a guarantee: a determined program, a flaw in the computer's software, or you approving a request or sharing a folder can still let something through.
# Help topic "AI programs": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-p2 = A fenced program sees an empty home folder. Your documents, ssh keys, keyring and browser logins are not there to read. It sees only the folders you share, at the same place as yours. The rest of the system stays visible, so it can read logs and run commands. Everything it starts, including programs it installs, stays inside the fence.
# Help topic "AI programs": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-step3-1 = To share a folder, right-click it in Folders and choose Share with AI programs, or use Share a folder on the AI programs page.
# Help topic "AI programs": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-step3-2 = Hidden folders, and folders that keep keys, passwords or mail, can never be shared.
# Help topic "AI programs": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-step3-3 = To reach another computer by ssh, add it on the page. Trindle uses a separate key, only for the computers you list.
# Help topic "AI programs": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-step3-4 = A command that needs the administrator opens a window outside the fence and waits for you.
# Help topic "AI programs": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-ai-programs-p4 = Only the names on the page start inside the fence. A program started some other way is not fenced. Turn the fence off on the page, and AI programs can see everything you can.

## Topic: Welcome to Trindle (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Welcome to Trindle".
settings-help-topics-welcome-title = Welcome to Trindle
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Welcome to Trindle" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-welcome-keywords = start begin new first introduction what is how edge open windows
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-welcome-p1 = Trindle keeps your screen clear. There is no bar and no menu along the edges: just your windows and your wallpaper. Everything else lives in one circle, the ring, which appears when you call it and goes away when you are done.
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": caption and spoken name of picture 2 (ring-first.png).
settings-help-topics-welcome-pic2 = The ring, with the time and date in the middle
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-welcome-p3 = Everything can be done with the mouse or a finger. These open the ring, where your windows are:
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-welcome-step4-1 = Move the pointer to the edge of the screen and double-click: the ring opens.
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-welcome-step4-2 = The Super key opens it too.
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-welcome-step4-3 = Your open windows are in the ring: click one to bring it to the front.
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-welcome-p5 = The Welcome, the short tour that opens the first time you log in, shows all of this with moving pictures. It starts by asking how much you know, and leaves out what you already do. It is in All apps as Welcome whenever you want to see it again.
# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-welcome-p6 = A button that could lose something, such as a bin, asks twice. The first click fills it with the highlight colour and shows a tick; click again to go ahead. Leave it, and after three seconds it goes back as it was.

## Topic: Opening the ring and finding apps (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps".
settings-help-topics-ring-title = Opening the ring and finding apps
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-ring-keywords = launcher tray icons brightness tools apps applications start menu open program edge click favourites favorites all apps categories office games type search find filter
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-ring-p1 = Move the pointer to the very edge of the screen, any edge, and double click: the ring appears. It works with a window filling the screen too, but never over a full-screen game or video, where every click belongs to the window. Just moving the pointer there never opens it, so it does not pop up by accident, and a click elsewhere on the desktop opens nothing. The Super key opens it as well, over a full-screen window too. A double click on the empty desktop opens it too.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-ring-p2 = In the middle are the time and date, and a row of small icons: sound, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and battery, the cup that keeps the screen awake, the keyboard on screen once it is switched on in Settings, Keyboard and mouse, and, on a computer with a backlight, the screen brightness (scroll over it to change it, click it for a slider). Below those are All apps, a button for updates when there are any, and Power, which has Lock, Screensaver, Suspend, Hibernate where the computer can, Log out, Restart and Shut down.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-ring-p3 = Click the sound, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi or battery icon in the middle and a round panel opens over the favourites: change the volume or the microphone, connect Bluetooth headphones, join a Wi-Fi network, or see how long the battery will last and how worn it is. The gear in the panel opens the full Settings page; the cross closes it.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-ring-p4 = Round the middle sit your favourite apps, with Settings, Apps and Folders always among them, and four tool circles drawn like apps: Screenshot (the camera), Clipboard, Notifications and the Magnifier. Click an app to open it, or to bring its window to the front. Programs that are open show in the ring of open windows round the favourites, not among the favourites.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-ring-p5 = Icons of other programs that are running, such as a chat app or a backup tool, sit in a row at the bottom of the middle. Click one to open its program; right-click it for its menu. A plugin's icon sits there too: click it to start it, or right-click it for what the plugin offers, such as Connect and Disconnect for Flow, and for its settings.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": caption and spoken name of picture 6 (ring-all.png).
settings-help-topics-ring-pic6 = All apps: every app round the outside, in pieces by kind
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-1 = Click All apps in the middle. Every app that is not already a favourite appears round the outside, in pieces: Internet, Office, Graphics, Sound and video, Games and so on. A kind with no apps left has no piece.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-2 = Click an app in any piece to open it.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-3 = Looking for one app? Just type a few letters of its name: only the apps that match stay, best first, and Enter opens the first. Escape clears what you typed.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-4 = Click All apps again to put the pieces away.
# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": tip 8, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-ring-tip8 = Press Escape, or click outside the circle, to close the ring.

## Topic: Keeping the screen awake (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Keeping the screen awake".
settings-help-topics-awake-title = Keeping the screen awake
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keeping the screen awake" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-awake-keywords = cup caffeine keep awake screen off sleep dim lock video presentation
# Help topic "Keeping the screen awake": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-awake-p1 = Watching something, or reading without touching the mouse? Click the cup in the middle of the ring. It lights up, and until you click it again or log out the screen does not dim, lock, turn off or sleep.
# Help topic "Keeping the screen awake": tip 2, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-awake-tip2 = The same switch is at the top of Settings, Power.

## Topic: Keeping your favourite apps in the ring (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring".
settings-help-topics-favourites-title = Keeping your favourite apps in the ring
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-favourites-keywords = favourite favourites favorite favorites keep add remove right-click drag move order
# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p1 = Right-click any app, in the ring or among All apps, and choose Add to favourites. It then always sits round the middle of the ring. Right-click a favourite and choose Remove from favourites to take it away. The middle holds twelve.
# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p2 = You start with Settings, Apps, Folders, Drives and Activity. Any of them can be taken off, and put back from All apps.
# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p3 = To put your favourites in a different order, drag one with the mouse and drop it on another's place; it takes that place and the rest move round.
# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": caption and spoken name of picture 4 (ring-menu.png).
settings-help-topics-favourites-pic4 = Right-click an app for more choices
# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p5 = The same menu can open another window of an app, close its windows, and, for apps installed from Apps, uninstall it.

## Topic: Switching between windows (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Switching between windows".
settings-help-topics-switcher-title = Switching between windows
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Switching between windows" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-switcher-keywords = windows switch switcher alt tab open ring desktop show minimise clipboard copy paste history keyboard on screen
# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p1 = Open the ring and your open windows are in a ring round the favourites, one part for each window, with its program's icon and its name. Point at one and the middle shows a picture of that window; click it to bring that window to the front, or right-click it for minimise, maximise and close. Alt and Tab also brings each open window to the front in turn.
# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p2 = Super and D puts every window away to show the desktop, and brings them back. With nothing open, the screen is clean.
# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p3 = The clipboard is one of the four tool circles among the favourites in the ring, and opens as a round panel, like the sound or Wi-Fi ones. It shows what you copied lately, newest first: type in the filter to find one, click one to copy it again, and pin the ones you want kept for good. The small cross beside a line forgets just that one, handy for a password copied by mistake, and the bin at the foot forgets everything but the pinned ones, and asks twice. Choosing a line only puts it back on the clipboard: you paste it yourself, where you mean it to go, so nothing ever lands in a box you did not choose. Settings, Ring, Clipboard chooses when the rest is forgotten: each restart, after a day, a week or two weeks, or never.
# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p4 = The keyboard on screen has its own small icon in the middle of the ring, once it is switched on in Settings, Keyboard and mouse, under Keyboard button in the ring. It opens a keyboard along the bottom of the screen, and the same icon closes it again.

## Topic: Notifications: what programs tell you (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you".
settings-help-topics-notifications-title = Notifications: what programs tell you
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-notifications-keywords = notification notifications alert message banner popup toast bell quiet silence do not disturb missed mail battery corner
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-notifications-p1 = When a program has something to tell you, such as an email arriving or the battery running low, it appears as a small circle in a corner of the screen, stays for a few seconds, and goes again. Up to three show at once, one above another. It sits in the corner, so nothing lands over what you are reading.
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-notifications-p2 = The Notifications circle in the ring opens everything that has been said, oldest at the top and the newest at the bottom, with the time beside each one. If you have been away from the computer there may be a great many, so the list opens at the bottom, where the newest is, and you scroll up for the older ones.
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-notifications-p3 = Beside each line are two small buttons. The pin keeps that one for good: it is gathered above the rest and forgetting everything leaves it alone. The small bin forgets just that one. At the foot are a bell button, described below, and a bin that forgets everything but the pinned ones and asks twice.
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-notifications-p4 = How long each one stays is yours to choose, in Settings, Ring, Notifications: three seconds for a glance, fifteen for time to read it, or until you close it. An urgent one always waits to be closed, so a warning cannot take itself away before it has been read.
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-notifications-p5 = To turn notifications off, open the list from the Notifications circle and press the bell at its foot. It crosses through while they are off, so you can see at a glance. They stop appearing, but they are still gathered in the list, so nothing is missed by turning them off for an hour. Press it again to turn them back on.
# Help topic "Notifications: what programs tell you": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-notifications-tip6 = The cross on a notification only puts it away. It is still in the list. The word Done after a screenshot is saved appears in the same place.

## Topic: Getting new apps (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Getting new apps".
settings-help-topics-apps-title = Getting new apps
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Getting new apps" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-apps-keywords = install store apps download software flathub browser firefox brave uninstall remove update deb package chrome vs code zoom driver double-click
# Help topic "Getting new apps": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-apps-p1 = Trindle does not choose your apps for you. The first time you log in, the Welcome shows a few pages of popular apps, a browser and the like; pick the ones you want and press Install the chosen apps. Welcome is in All apps whenever you want those pages again.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-apps-p2 = Later, open Apps from the ring. Browse by kind on the left, or search at the top. Click an app to read about it, then press the download arrow to install it. Its stars show under its name, pictures of it scroll across under the install button, and What people say, further down, has its reviews; the globe button opens its page on Flathub.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": caption and spoken name of picture 3 (store-app.png).
settings-help-topics-apps-pic3 = An app's page in Apps
# Help topic "Getting new apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-apps-step4-1 = To remove an app, find it in Apps and press the bin twice: the first press fills it with the highlight colour and shows a tick, and the second removes it.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-apps-step4-2 = Or right-click it in the ring and choose Uninstall.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-apps-step4-3 = Updates appear under Updates in Apps' list; press the update button.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": tip 5, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-apps-tip5 = Apps are installed for you only, so no password is needed.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-apps-p6 = Some apps are not on Flathub and are published on GitHub instead, as an AppImage or a Flatpak file. Choose From GitHub in Apps' list and type a name: AppImages listed on AppImageHub appear as you type, and pressing Enter searches the whole of GitHub. The eye shows the files in an app's newest release; the download arrow installs it straight away.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": tip 7, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-apps-tip7 = Nobody checks apps from GitHub the way Flathub checks its own. Install only from people you trust.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": paragraph 8.
settings-help-topics-apps-p8 = The small round key button at the far end of the Search GitHub card opens a box for a GitHub key. You almost certainly do not need one. Without it GitHub answers 60 questions an hour from your computer; with one it answers 5,000, which only matters if you are searching a great deal. Trindle keeps what GitHub has answered for a few hours, so searching for the same thing again uses none of them, and if the limit is reached the page says when GitHub will answer again. The key lets Trindle look at public files and nothing else: it gives no way into your account, and you tick no permissions when you make it.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": paragraph 9.
settings-help-topics-apps-p9 = Some makers offer their program as a Debian package instead, a file ending in .deb: Google Chrome, VS Code, Zoom and many printer drivers come this way. Once it has downloaded, double-click it in Folders, or open it from the browser's downloads. A small window says what it is, its version and whether you have it already; press the download arrow and give your password, and anything else it needs comes in with it. If it has an app, the app appears in the ring.
# Help topic "Getting new apps": tip 10, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-apps-tip10 = A package can change anything on the computer, so install only ones from makers you trust.

## Topic: Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more".
settings-help-topics-sources-title = Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-sources-keywords = software sources repository repo apt claude anthropic chatgpt openai codex ai tailscale syncthing jellyfin media server node nodejs github gh docker deb newer
# Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-sources-p1 = A few programs are not on Flathub and come from their makers' own Debian sources instead, or come in much newer versions from there: Claude, Tailscale, Syncthing, Jellyfin, Node.js, GitHub CLI and Docker. Anything on Flathub, browsers included, comes from Apps as usual. In Apps, choose Software sources and press the plus beside one. After your administrator's password, the source is added and the program installed; from then on it is updated with Debian's updates.
# Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-sources-p2 = A source can install updates for the whole computer, so add only makers you trust. The bin removes the program and its source together.
# Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-sources-p3 = For Tailscale, press the sign-in button once it is installed: Tailscale's sign-in page opens in the browser, and Apps says when you are connected.
# Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-sources-tip4 = Add another source takes any maker's source from their install instructions: its address, suite, components and signing key.

## Topic: Undoing an update with snapshots (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots".
settings-help-topics-snapshots-title = Undoing an update with snapshots
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-keywords = snapshots snapshot restore undo rollback roll back update broke boot menu grub btrfs
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-p1 = When the system is on Btrfs, a snapshot of it is taken before and after every software change: installing, updating or removing anything from Debian or a software source. Settings, Snapshots lists them. The camera takes one now, before trying something you may want to undo.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-step2-1 = Open Settings and choose Snapshots.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-step2-2 = Press the restore arrow beside the snapshot from before things went wrong, and again to be sure.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-step2-3 = Restart. The system is as it was then; what it was just now is set aside, not deleted.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-p3 = If the computer will not start, choose Trindle snapshots in the boot menu, then a snapshot. It starts as it was then, and nothing done there is kept; restore it in Settings to keep it.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-tip4 = The boot menu is hidden. Tap Esc once as the computer starts to show it. Holding Esc goes past it to a typing screen: type normal and press Enter to get the menu back.
# Help topic "Undoing an update with snapshots": tip 5, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-snapshots-tip5 = When Trindle was put on with its own installer, your home folder has a part of the disk of its own and is left out of the snapshots, so restoring never changes your files. Settings, Snapshots says so if this computer is laid out differently.

## Topic: Moving to another computer (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Moving to another computer".
settings-help-topics-moving-title = Moving to another computer
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Moving to another computer" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-moving-keywords = backup back up restore move new computer transfer copy settings apps data flat fmpack
# Help topic "Moving to another computer": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-moving-p1 = In Apps, choose Move your apps. Switch on what to take: your apps, their own data, your AppImages and Trindle's settings. Press the save button and one backup file is made in your home folder; copy it to a USB stick or cloud storage.
# Help topic "Moving to another computer": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-moving-p2 = On the other computer, open the same page, press the open button and choose the file. The apps are installed fresh from Flathub, and their data and your settings are put back. Log out and back in to see every setting.
# Help topic "Moving to another computer": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-moving-p3 = The backup is the same kind of file Trindle Flat makes (it ends in .fmpack), so Trindle Flat can open it on any Linux computer, and a backup or app list made by Trindle Flat can be chosen here. Its name on the page is a link to its website. Apps already on this computer keep their own settings; only apps it installs get theirs back.
# Help topic "Moving to another computer": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-moving-tip4 = Your documents, pictures and other files are not in the backup: copy those over yourself. Wi-Fi passwords and your GitHub key stay behind too.

## Topic: Connecting to Wi-Fi (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Connecting to Wi-Fi".
settings-help-topics-wifi-title = Connecting to Wi-Fi
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Connecting to Wi-Fi" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-wifi-keywords = wifi wi-fi wireless internet network connect password ethernet cable
# Help topic "Connecting to Wi-Fi": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 1.
settings-help-topics-wifi-step1-1 = Open the ring and click the network icon in the middle, or open Settings and choose Network.
# Help topic "Connecting to Wi-Fi": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 1.
settings-help-topics-wifi-step1-2 = Make sure the Wi-Fi switch is on.
