# Help topic "Screen size and displays": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-display-p1 = If things look too small or too large, open Settings and choose Display, then change Size. 100% is the comfortable standard size for your screen.
# Help topic "Screen size and displays": caption and spoken name of picture 2 (settings-display.png).
settings-help-topics-display-pic2 = The Display page
# Help topic "Screen size and displays": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-display-p3 = After a change, a message asks whether to keep it. If you do nothing for fifteen seconds the screen goes back to how it was, so a wrong choice can never leave you stuck.
# Help topic "Screen size and displays": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-display-p4 = Night light, at the foot of the same page, turns the screen warmer in the evening, with less of the blue light that keeps eyes awake. Switch it on and it follows sunset and sunrise, worked out from your time zone, so nothing asks where you are. Choose Set times instead to pick when it starts and ends, and move Warmth to the right for a warmer screen.

## Topic: NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster".
settings-help-topics-nvidia-title = NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster
# Words people might search for the Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-keywords = nvidia graphics card driver nouveau open slow speed gpu secure boot proprietary geforce
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-p1 = Trindle starts on every NVIDIA card using the open driver that comes with Linux, called nouveau. Nothing has to be installed and nothing has to be decided: the desktop works from the first start.
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-p2 = On an older card that is the end of the story. On a card from roughly 2022 onwards the open driver cannot raise the card's speed above idle, so the desktop still works but feels sluggish and video may stutter. NVIDIA's own driver fixes that.
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-tip3 = If your computer feels fine, leave it alone. Changing a graphics driver is the most common way to end up with a screen that will not come on.
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-p4 = To install NVIDIA's own driver, open the Terminal from the ring and type:
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-step5-1 = sudo apt update
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-step5-2 = sudo apt install nvidia-driver
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-step5-3 = Do not restart yet: read the next part first.
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-p6 = Debian builds the driver for your kernel as it installs, which takes a few minutes and pulls in a compiler. It rebuilds itself whenever the kernel is updated.
# Help topic "NVIDIA graphics, and making them faster": paragraph 10.
settings-help-topics-nvidia-p10 = To go back to the open driver at any time: sudo apt remove nvidia-driver, then restart.

## Topic: Making the screen bigger where you are looking (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking".
settings-help-topics-magnifier-title = Making the screen bigger where you are looking
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-keywords = magnifier magnify zoom bigger larger reading glasses eyesight sight small print loupe accessibility
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-p1 = The magnifier shows a circle of magnified screen wherever you press, so you can read small print without changing anything about the screen itself.
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-step2-1 = Click the Magnifier circle in the ring.
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-step2-2 = Hold a finger, or the mouse button, anywhere on the screen. A circle appears with everything under it made larger.
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-step2-3 = Keep holding and move about: the circle follows, so you can read along a line.
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-step2-4 = Let go and the circle disappears. Press the button again when you have finished.
# Help topic "Making the screen bigger where you are looking": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-magnifier-tip3 = While the magnifier is on, the screen underneath is held still and cannot be pressed, so you cannot set anything off by accident while you are reading.

## Topic: Picking a colour from the screen (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Picking a colour from the screen".
