## Topic: Colours, icons and text (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Colours, icons and text".
settings-help-topics-look-title = Colours, icons and text
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Colours, icons and text" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-look-keywords = colour colours color colors accent theme dark light mode sunshine bright font text icons cursor pointer look
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-look-p1 = Open Settings and choose Appearance. Under Theme, a theme that has both a dark look and a light one has two buttons beside its picture, Dark and Light. Press Light when the screen is hard to see in bright sunshine, and Dark again later; everything changes at once, apps installed from Apps the next time they open. A theme with one look, such as Midnight, which is dark, or Paper, which is light, has no such buttons: to change between dark and light there, choose another theme.
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-look-p2 = The highlight colour is used for buttons, switches and wherever you are; pick one of the twenty colours or type your own.
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": caption and spoken name of picture 3 (settings-appearance.png).
settings-help-topics-look-pic3 = The Appearance page
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-look-p4 = Under Icons, the Trindle Icons are circles: each app's own mark in white on a colour of its own, the same in light and in dark. An app the set does not draw yet gets its own picture on a white circle, so it still fits in.
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-look-p5 = The same page changes the font and the size of the pointer, and can take a font of your own; see adding fonts.
# Help topic "Colours, icons and text": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-look-p6 = A theme changes the whole look at once; see themes. To change more by hand, such as shadows, corners or Apps' background, see changing the look with a style sheet.

## Topic: Adding fonts of your own (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Adding fonts of your own".
settings-help-topics-themes-title = Adding fonts of your own
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Adding fonts of your own" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-themes-keywords = font fonts ttf otf ttc zip add upload install typeface letters
# Help topic "Adding fonts of your own": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-themes-p1 = Settings, Appearance can take a font you have downloaded, so you are not left with the ones that come with Trindle. Add it under Text, and it appears in the Font list there.
# Help topic "Adding fonts of your own": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-themes-p2 = A font is a .ttf, .otf or .ttc file, or a zip holding some. Every font found is copied into your own fonts folder and is then in the Font list. Fonts for the whole computer, for everyone who uses it, are installed from Apps instead.
# Help topic "Adding fonts of your own": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-themes-tip3 = Nothing you add is sent anywhere, and nothing already on the computer is replaced.

## Topic: Themes: a whole look from one file (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file".
settings-help-topics-theme-files-title = Themes: a whole look from one file
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-keywords = theme themes look skin file css add share make own glass dark light keep go back undo thirty seconds footer safe default trial
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p1 = A theme is a whole look, colours, corners, sizes and room, kept in one file. Settings, Appearance, Theme shows every theme as a small picture drawn from its own colours. Trindle's own look, a deep blue night, is always first in the list and can never be removed. Midnight, which comes with Trindle, is black and grey with no blue in it, as Trindle's own look used to be.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-step2-1 = Press a theme to try it. Every Trindle window changes at once. A theme that has both a dark look and a light one has Dark and Light beside its picture: press one of those instead to try the theme in that look. On the theme you already use they change the look straight away, with nothing to keep.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-step2-2 = Keep is asked in a box along the bottom of the window, which stays in sight however far down the page you are. Press Keep within 30 seconds to keep it. If you do nothing, or press Go back now or Escape, the look you had comes back by itself, everywhere, even if Settings has been closed.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-step2-3 = To add a theme somebody has shared, press Add a theme and choose its .css file. It is checked first, and one that is not a usable theme is refused with the line and the reason.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p3 = A theme file is written like Trindle's own sheet (see changing the look with a style sheet): a :root { "{" } { "}" } block holding any of the same names, such as --trindle-page: #101820;. At its top it can say who it is, with --trindle-theme-name, --trindle-theme-about (one line), --trindle-theme-author and --trindle-theme-version, and which looks it has with --trindle-theme-look: dark; light; or both;. Whatever it leaves out stays as Trindle has it. Themes you add are kept in the themes folder inside the trindle folder in your .config folder, and removing one from there removes it from the list.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p4 = A theme with both looks keeps them in the one file. Its dark values go in :root { "{" } { "}" } as usual, and its light ones in a second block, @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) { "{" } :root { "{" } { "}" } { "}" }, exactly as Trindle's own sheet does. Give the light block every colour and shadow the dark look sets, or the dark ones show through in the light look. Settings then shows Dark and Light beside the theme's picture. A theme that says dark or light has that one look, is always drawn in it, and has no such buttons; one that does not say is taken to have both when it has a light block and to be dark when it has none. Other programs are told the look your theme is really drawn in, so a light theme makes them light.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p5 = A theme is safe to try, whoever made it. It is only ever read as a list of values, never run, and it cannot bring in another file or reach the internet. Only values Trindle knows are taken, each kept within its limits, so no theme can hide a button, a question or a warning, or make text too small to read. Words are kept clear enough to read whatever colours a theme gives, red stays red, and your highlight colour stays your own. A theme changes only your own windows: the login screen always wears Trindle's own look.
# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-tip6 = If a look ever goes wrong and you cannot find your way back, open a terminal, or a text console with Ctrl, Alt and F3, and type: trindle-settings --theme default

## Topic: Changing the look with a style sheet (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet".
settings-help-topics-theming-title = Changing the look with a style sheet
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-theming-keywords = theme theming style sheet stylesheet css colours colors shadows corners rounder bigger text size buttons background page store look custom own retheme
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-theming-p1 = Everything Trindle draws, its colours, shadows, corners, the room between things, the size of buttons and the size and weight of words, comes from one style sheet, written the way web pages are styled. You, or an AI you ask, can change any of it with a sheet of your own.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theming-step2-1 = Make a file called style.css in the trindle folder inside your .config folder (Folders shows it once hidden files are shown).
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theming-step2-2 = Put in only what you want to change, inside :root { "{" } { "}" }. For example, --trindle-page: #101010; gives every window a near-black page, --trindle-highlight: #4bae4f; makes buttons green, and --trindle-card-corner: 16; makes cards rounder.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-theming-step2-3 = Save it. Every Trindle window, the ring and the title bars change straight away.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-theming-p3 = Every name you can use is in Trindle's own sheet, /usr/share/trindle/style.css, with what it does and its limits beside it, such as (6 to 18). Changes for the light look alone go in a @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) { "{" } :root { "{" } { "}" } { "}" } block, as in Trindle's sheet. Shadows are written as a web page writes them, such as 0 8px 20px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8). Cards cast a gentle shadow to begin with; --trindle-card-shadow: none; makes them flat, and --trindle-edge: transparent; takes their fine edge away.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-theming-p4 = Nothing you write can break a window. Names Trindle does not know are ignored, a value it cannot read is replaced by Trindle's own, and sizes are kept within their limits. Colours are up to you: a colour you set is left exactly as you wrote it, and Trindle moves its own words so they can still be read on a page, a card or a title bar of yours. A colour set for the light look alone is yours in the light look only. A see-through colour is measured over black and over white, as what shows through it is not known; where your own colours leave nothing that can be read on all of them, Trindle's words go as far as they can, to black or to white. Three things stay readable whatever a sheet says: red words that tell of a failure, the words on your highlight colour, and the boxes that ask for a password, which are always drawn in Trindle's own look. When colours you set fall short of what can be read, Settings, Appearance says "Some colours are hard to read." and leaves them as you wrote them. If words you set yourself become hard to read, delete the file and Trindle's own look comes back at once. The file must be an ordinary file of words, at most 256 KB; one that is not is left unused, and Settings, Appearance says so.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-theming-p5 = A sheet of your own is laid over whichever theme is chosen, so a small change of yours stays when you change theme. For a whole look to keep or share, make a theme instead; see themes.
# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-theming-tip6 = A colour set in your sheet with --trindle-highlight wins over the highlight colour chosen in Settings, Appearance.

## Topic: Voice control (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Voice control".
settings-help-topics-voice-title = Voice control
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Voice control" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-voice-keywords = voice speak talk microphone commands whisper listen say
# Help topic "Voice control": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-voice-p1 = Voice control is an optional extra. To add it, open Settings, choose Ring, and press Add voice. It takes a few minutes and about 150 MB.
# Help topic "Voice control": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-voice-p2 = Then open the ring, tap its middle anywhere there is no button, and speak. It stops listening by itself when you stop talking, or tap again. It works with a finger as well as the mouse. Things you can say:
# Help topic "Voice control": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-1 = An app's name, such as “Firefox” or “folders”.
# Help topic "Voice control": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-2 = “Switch to” an app, or “close window”.
# Help topic "Voice control": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-3 = “Louder”, “quieter”, “mute”.
# Help topic "Voice control": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-4 = “Wi-Fi settings”, “battery settings”, or any other settings page.
# Help topic "Voice control": step 5 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-5 = “Install” and an app's name, to look for it in Apps.
# Help topic "Voice control": step 6 of the numbered steps at block 3.
settings-help-topics-voice-step3-6 = “Next wallpaper”, “screenshot”, “what time is it”.
# Help topic "Voice control": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-voice-tip4 = Your voice is understood on this computer. Nothing is sent anywhere. Voice can be switched off on the Ring page without removing it.

## Topic: Folders, USB sticks and phones (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones".
settings-help-topics-files-title = Folders, USB sticks and phones
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-files-keywords = folders files file manager usb stick drive phone camera nas network share sharing samba disks eject format extract unpack zip compress archive rar 7z tar iso disc image password terminal f4 root wallpaper copy path location preview space bar emblem badge resize rotate picture columns music photo pdf keys keyboard shortcuts tab tabs new window
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-files-p1 = Folders is the file manager, one of the apps round the ring. Plugged-in USB sticks, phones and cameras appear on its left, and so do shared folders on your home network under Network.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-files-p2 = Folders opens with one pane showing your Home folder, with Downloads and Documents pinned on the left. The Single and Dual switch at the top left (or F3) adds a second pane, which shows Recent unless you chose another place in Folders' own settings, behind the cog at the end of its top bar (or Ctrl and comma): where each pane starts, tick boxes for picking, file endings, and whether a drive is opened as it is plugged in. Each pane has its own row of tabs above its list. The top bar keeps only what changes how the folder looks, plus Undo and New folder; everything that acts on what you picked, such as Rename, Cut, Copy, Move to Trash, Delete permanently and Wipe securely, is in the right-click menu, and each still has its key.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-files-p3 = In the right-click menu, Open with another app lists the apps that can open the file: double-click one, or press Enter on it, to open the file with it at once. The tick does the same.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-files-p4 = When you plug in a USB stick or a memory card, the ring says so with one line and the word Open. Open shows its files in Folders, asking for the passphrase first if it is locked. Folders lists it under Drives whether or not you press Open, mounted or not, with a round Eject button that puts it away safely. In "Open drives when plugged in", in Folders' own settings under Drives, does the opening for you. A drive plugged in while the screen is locked waits until you unlock it.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-files-p5 = Right-click in Folders for more: Extract here and Extract to a folder for archives, Compress to make a .zip or a .tar, Open in Terminal, Open as Root, Copy location, and Set as wallpaper on a picture. Folders opens archives itself: double-click a .zip, .7z, .rar or .tar, a disc image (.iso) or almost any other kind and it opens like a folder, so you can look inside and take out only what you want. A .zip or .7z locked with a password asks for it; Folders does not keep the password, so it asks again the next time. A .rar locked with a password cannot be opened, and Folders tells you so.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-files-p6 = Press the space bar on a file to look at it without opening it, and again to close it. F4 opens a terminal in the folder you are in. Right-click a folder or a file and choose Pin to the ring to keep it round the middle of the ring with your favourite apps, where one click opens it; right-click it there to unpin it. Right-click pictures to resize or rotate them. A file's Properties can give it a small emblem, and a folder's can share it with other computers on your home network: the first time, Folders puts Samba on, which asks for your password, and sharing works once the computer has restarted. In the list view, right-click the column headings to add music, photo and PDF details.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 7.
settings-help-topics-files-p7 = Each name in the path along the top has a small arrow after it: press it for a list of the folders inside that folder, and choose one to go there. A middle click on a folder, or Ctrl and a double click, opens it in a new tab behind the one you are on, and a middle click on a tab closes it. Drag a folder between two of the pinned folders on the left to pin it there, and drag the pinned folders up and down to put them in the order you like. Under the Find box are three lists, Kind, Size and Changed, that narrow what is shown as soon as you choose.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 8.
settings-help-topics-files-p8 = The keys are the usual ones for a file manager. Alt and Left go back, Alt and Right forward, and Alt and Up or Backspace up one folder. Ctrl and L, or Alt and D, let you type a place. Ctrl and T opens a tab, Ctrl and W closes it (and the window, when it is the last), Ctrl and Tab moves to the next tab and Ctrl, Shift and Tab to the one before. Ctrl and N opens another window. Ctrl and F finds, F2 renames, F5 reads the folder again, Delete moves to the Trash and Shift and Delete deletes permanently. Ctrl, Shift and N makes a folder. Ctrl and A selects all, Ctrl, Shift and A none, and Ctrl and I inverts the selection. Alt and Enter opens Properties. F3 switches between one pane and two, and F4 opens a terminal.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 9.
settings-help-topics-files-p9 = Drives, in All apps and in Settings, About this computer, shows every drive and how much space is free. From there you can open a USB stick, eject it safely before pulling it out, or format it. The drive the system runs on is never offered for formatting.
# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": caption and spoken name of picture 10 (settings-disks.png).
settings-help-topics-files-pic10 = The Drives window

## Topic: Apps that come as an AppImage (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Apps that come as an AppImage".
settings-help-topics-appimages-title = Apps that come as an AppImage
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Apps that come as an AppImage" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-appimages-keywords = appimage app image download portable website install
# Help topic "Apps that come as an AppImage": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-appimages-p1 = Some apps are downloaded from their makers' websites as a single file ending in .AppImage. Open Apps, choose Installed and press the plus beside Add an AppImage, then pick the file. It is copied into the .appimage folder in your home, and appears in the ring with its own name and picture.
# Help topic "Apps that come as an AppImage": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-appimages-p2 = It is listed on the same page with every other app; to remove it, press its bin twice. The copy you downloaded is left where it was.

## Topic: Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps".
settings-help-topics-older-programs-title = Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-older-programs-keywords = older programs x11 xwayland steam wine games java old apps allow switch watch spy
# Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-older-programs-p1 = Most apps on Trindle draw their windows the newer way. Some older programs, such as Steam and its games, Wine games and Java apps, still draw them the older way, through a helper called Xwayland. On other desktops every older program shares one helper, so any of them can watch the others' windows and what you type into them.
# Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-older-programs-p2 = Trindle gives each older app you installed in its own folder, and each Flathub app that needs it, a helper of its own, so none can see another. Programs installed for the whole computer share one. A helper only starts when an older program opens and stops after it closes, so it uses no memory the rest of the time.
# Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-older-programs-tip3 = If you never use older programs, you can turn them off in Settings, Permissions, with Allow older programs. While it is off they do not open.

