# Help topic "Apps that start by themselves": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-startup-step2-1 = To add one, press the grid button and pick from the apps installed, or press the terminal button to type a command or choose a program on the disk.
# Help topic "Apps that start by themselves": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-startup-step2-2 = Each has a delay: At once, or after a few seconds, for an app that needs the network or another app to be ready first.
# Help topic "Apps that start by themselves": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-startup-step2-3 = The switch keeps an app in the list but stops it starting; the bin takes it away.

## Topic: Keyboard shortcuts (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts".
settings-help-topics-keys-title = Keyboard shortcuts
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-keys-keywords = keys keyboard shortcuts super windows key alt tab screenshot print volume brightness lock terminal close maximise screen reader desktop show
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-keys-p1 = Everything can be done with the mouse; these keys are only quicker ways. Super is the key with the Windows sign on most keyboards.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-1 = Super on its own, or Super and Space: open or close the ring.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-2 = Super and Enter, or Ctrl, Alt and T: a Terminal.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-3 = Super and I: Settings.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-4 = Super and L: lock the screen, once locking is switched on in Settings, Power.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 5 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-5 = Super, Alt and S: turn reading the screen aloud on or off.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 6 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-6 = Super and Q: close the window in front.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 7 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-7 = Super and Up: make the window fill the screen, or go back.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 8 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-8 = Super and Down: tuck the window away; the ring's open windows bring it back.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 9 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-9 = Super and D: put every window away to show the desktop, and bring them back.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 10 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-10 = Super and N: move the keyboard into the notifications on the screen; Tab moves between their buttons, Enter or Space presses one, Escape leaves.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 11 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-11 = Alt and Tab: bring each open window to the front in turn.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 12 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-12 = Print, or Super, Shift and S: a picture of the screen, or of a part of it; Shift and Print: the whole screen at once.
# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 13 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-13 = The volume, mute and brightness keys work as marked.

## Topic: Drives and USB sticks (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Drives and USB sticks".
settings-help-topics-disks-title = Drives and USB sticks
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Drives and USB sticks" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-disks-keywords = drives disk disks drive usb stick memory card format wipe erase eject power off space full storage partition part resize shrink grow health smart check repair mount start-up startup fstab name folder mnt
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-disks-p1 = Drives is a window of its own, in All apps, and Settings, About this computer has a way into it too. It says in one sentence at the top how much room is left, with a ring for each part in use, and shows every drive in or plugged into this computer, how full each part of it is and, where the drive can tell, how healthy it is and how warm.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-disks-step2-1 = Plug in a USB stick; it appears in the list.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-disks-step2-2 = Press the folder to see its files, and the play and stop buttons to mount and unmount it.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-disks-step2-3 = Press eject before pulling it out, so nothing is lost. The power button turns off a USB drive that can be turned off, ready to unplug.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-disks-p3 = The pencil beside a part opens what can be changed about it:
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-1 = Name: what it is called here and on other computers.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-2 = Folder: where its files appear, such as /mnt/backup, instead of a folder named after its number. Leave it empty to let the computer choose.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-3 = Mount at start-up: ready whenever the computer starts. If the drive is not there, the computer starts anyway.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-4 = Size: make it smaller, or larger into free space just after it.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 5 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-5 = Faults: look for faults and put them right. It is unmounted while this runs.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": step 6 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-disks-step4-6 = Format, and Delete this part.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-disks-p5 = The three dots beside a drive erase the whole drive and start it afresh as one part. Free space has a pencil too, to make a new part in it. Choose exFAT for a drive shared with Windows and Macs, ext4 for one used only with Linux, and FAT for a small stick or an older device.
# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-disks-tip6 = Formatting, resizing and deleting ask twice, naming the drive and its size, and ask for your password. The drive the computer runs from is never offered for any of them. Erasing cannot be undone: copy anything you want to keep first.

## Topic: Which app opens what (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Which app opens what".
settings-help-topics-defaults-title = Which app opens what
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Which app opens what" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-defaults-keywords = default apps browser email open with pdf pictures music video links
# Help topic "Which app opens what": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-defaults-p1 = Settings, Default apps chooses which app opens web links, emails, pictures, music, videos, PDF files and folders. Only apps that are installed and can open that kind of thing are offered.
# Help topic "Which app opens what": tip 2, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-defaults-tip2 = No web browser comes with Trindle. Get one from Apps first, and it can then be chosen here.

## Topic: Keeping the computer up to date (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date".
settings-help-topics-updates-title = Keeping the computer up to date
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-updates-keywords = update updates upgrade security new version debian trindle install firmware bios fwupd
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-updates-p1 = Everything is updated from one place: Apps' Updates page.
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-updates-step2-1 = Open Apps and choose Updates. It lists your apps, Debian and Trindle itself, with the number waiting shown on the Apps button in the ring.
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-updates-step2-2 = Press the update button on each card. Debian and Trindle ask for your password; apps do not.
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-updates-step2-3 = Log out and back in after a Trindle update, so the new version is the one running.
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-updates-p3 = Now and then the makers of the computer, or of one of its parts such as a dock or a touchpad, put out new firmware: the programs built into the hardware itself. When there is some for yours, a Firmware card appears on the same page with the new version beside each part, and its update button asks for your password. The computer may need to restart to finish, so keep your work saved and, on a laptop, the charger plugged in. When there is nothing new, the card is not there at all.
# Help topic "Keeping the computer up to date": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-updates-tip4 = Nothing installs itself: updates go in only when you press Update, in the ring or Apps. Your settings, apps and files are kept through every update.

## Topic: About Trindle (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "About Trindle".
settings-help-topics-about-title = About Trindle
# Words people might search for the Help topic "About Trindle" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-about-keywords = about version what is debian compositor wayland smithay rust licence license gpl warranty risk who made
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-about-p1 = Trindle is a small desktop for Debian 13. All of its own parts are written in Rust: its compositor, which puts every window on the screen, and one program for everything else, the ring, Settings, Apps, the login and lock screens.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-about-p2 = GTK programs draw their own title bars. For the rest, such as the terminal, Qt programs and older X11 ones, the compositor draws the title bar itself, with round buttons at the right as GTK's own windows have: a line to put the window away, a square to fill the screen, and a cross to close it.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-about-p3 = Trindle is a rolling release: there is no timetable of big versions twice a year. Improvements come as they are ready, each with its own number, which Settings, About this computer shows along with the computer's processor, memory, graphics and the rest. That page is also where the computer's name is changed, and it keeps the last ten start-up times, from the power button to the login screen, with the whole list saved to Downloads at a click. What the computer is doing right now is in Activity, a window of its own.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-about-p4 = Older programs written for X11 rather than Wayland work too: the X server they need, Xwayland, starts the moment the first of them opens, and uses no memory until then.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-about-p5 = It is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 3: you may use, change and share it. It comes with no warranty of any kind and is used at your own risk; keep a backup of everything that matters.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-about-p6 = The website is trindle.org, and the code is at trindle.org/code.
# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 7.
settings-help-topics-about-p7 = Settings, Thanks lists the projects Trindle is built on, with a link to each one's home, and a heart for those that accept donations.

## Topic: What is running, and why it is busy (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy".
settings-help-topics-activity-title = What is running, and why it is busy
# Words people might search for the Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-activity-keywords = activity task manager system monitor running programs processes processor cpu memory ram busy slow fan hot threads stop close frozen not responding bubbles swap temperature
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-activity-p1 = Activity is a window of its own that shows what the computer is doing right now. Open it from All apps, under System, or from Settings, About this computer.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-activity-p2 = A sentence at the top says how things are, such as All calm, or which program is keeping the computer busy. Below it the computer is drawn as a room. The big circle is the memory, and each program is a bubble in it, as big as the memory it holds. A program that is working glows and beats, faster and brighter the harder it works: blue for a little work, orange for hard work and red for flat out. Round the edge is a bar for each of the processor's threads, which grows and warms as it gets busier.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-activity-p3 = All the parts of a program are added together, so Firefox is one bubble however many tabs it has. Two quiet grey bubbles hold the rest: Trindle desktop, which is everything Trindle runs to be the desktop, and Linux and other programs. Trindle's apps, such as Settings and Apps, count as programs, not as the desktop.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-activity-p4 = Down the side, four rings show the memory, the processor, the disk and the swap file; on a computer with no swap file the last one shows the processor's temperature instead. In the memory ring, Trindle's own share is in the highlight colour, and your programs and Linux are in blue.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-activity-step5-1 = If a program has stopped answering, click its bubble. A card opens beside it with what it is doing in plain figures.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-activity-step5-2 = Press the stop button on the card. It fills with the highlight colour and shows a tick.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 5.
settings-help-topics-activity-step5-3 = Press it again within three seconds. The program is asked to close, and anything not saved in it may be lost.
# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-activity-tip6 = Trindle's own programs and the system's have no stop button.

## Topic: Trying Trindle in a virtual machine (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Trying Trindle in a virtual machine".
settings-help-topics-virtual-machine-title = Trying Trindle in a virtual machine
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Trying Trindle in a virtual machine" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-virtual-machine-keywords = virtual machine vm virtualbox boxes qemu kvm vmware hyper-v parallels utm slow 3d acceleration graphics
# Help topic "Trying Trindle in a virtual machine": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-virtual-machine-p1 = Trindle runs in a virtual machine, and says so kindly when it starts in one: in the welcome window on the stick, and once in a notification after installing. On a real computer it never mentions it.
# Help topic "Trying Trindle in a virtual machine": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-virtual-machine-p2 = A virtual machine made without 3D graphics has no graphics chip for Trindle to use, so the processor draws everything instead. Windows, videos and web pages feel slower than they would on a real computer, and Trindle leaves out its animations to help. Turning on 3D acceleration in the virtual machine's display settings makes a big difference: in VirtualBox it is Enable 3D Acceleration, in GNOME Boxes and virt-manager 3D acceleration or Virtio with OpenGL.
# Help topic "Trying Trindle in a virtual machine": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-virtual-machine-tip3 = Judge Trindle's speed on a real computer: that is where it is meant to run.

## Topic: If something goes wrong (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "If something goes wrong".
settings-help-topics-trouble-title = If something goes wrong
# Words people might search for the Help topic "If something goes wrong" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-trouble-keywords = problem help stuck frozen crash login error broken recover terminal
# Help topic "If something goes wrong": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-trouble-p1 = If an app stops answering, right-click it in the ring and choose Close window. If that does not work, or the computer is slow and you do not know why, open Activity: it shows what is busy, and its stop button closes a program.
# Help topic "If something goes wrong": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-trouble-p2 = If the login screen ever fails to appear, or logging in keeps returning you to it, press Ctrl, Alt and F2 together. An ordinary text login appears, where you can log in and fix things; Ctrl, Alt and F7 returns to the graphical screen.
# Help topic "If something goes wrong": tip 3, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-trouble-tip3 = Trindle keeps a record of each session in .local/state/trindle/session.log in your home folder, which helps work out what went wrong.

## Topic: A chip with no driver (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "A chip with no driver".
settings-help-topics-hardware-no-driver-title = A chip with no driver or firmware
# Words people might search for the Help topic "A chip with no driver" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-hardware-no-driver-keywords = driver firmware wifi wi-fi bluetooth sound graphics chip missing no driver mediatek mt7902 not working usb stick
# Help topic "A chip with no driver": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-hardware-no-driver-p1 = A chip needs two things to work: a driver in the kernel, which is the core of Linux, and for many chips a firmware file, a small program the chip loads when it starts. Trindle looks at your computer's chips when you open Settings and says here when one has neither.
# Help topic "A chip with no driver": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-hardware-no-driver-p2 = A very new chip may have no driver in the kernel Debian ships. The MediaTek MT7902 is one: its Wi-Fi driver first appears in Linux 7.1, and its Bluetooth firmware is in firmware-mediatek 20260916. Trindle ships only Debian's stable kernel, so this chip stays without Wi-Fi until that kernel gains the driver.
# Title of the Help topic "A Bluetooth device will not connect".
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-title = A Bluetooth device will not connect
# Words people might search for the Help topic "A Bluetooth device will not connect" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-keywords = bluetooth connect connecting forget pair again headphones not working paired before old pairing key authentication
# Help topic "A Bluetooth device will not connect": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-p1 = Switch the device on and keep it near. If it was paired before and will not connect, or connects and drops at once, the pairing saved on this computer may no longer match the one in the device. This happens after the device is reset or paired with another computer.
# Help topic "A Bluetooth device will not connect": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-p2 = Press Forget and pair again beside the device, or the bin to forget it alone. Then put the device into pairing mode, as its instructions say, and press Pair beside it when it shows under devices nearby.
# Help topic "A Bluetooth device will not connect": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-connect-p3 = If it still fails, the sentence under the device says why: Bluetooth switched off, no answer from the device, a refused number, or a sound profile that is not ready. A device that says nothing for half a minute is not answering. Switch it off and on, then try again.
# Title of the Help topic "Headphones sound basic".
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-codec-title = Headphones sound basic
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Headphones sound basic" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-codec-keywords = bluetooth sound quality codec headphones basic sbc switched off on
# Help topic "Headphones sound basic": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-codec-p1 = When Bluetooth is switched off and on, the sound system can miss the better ways of sending sound to headphones, and falls back to a basic one. Trindle restarts your sound system's Bluetooth part for you a moment after Bluetooth comes back.
# Help topic "Headphones sound basic": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-codec-p2 = If the restart could not be done, log out and in again and the better quality returns.
# Help topic "A chip with no driver": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-hardware-no-driver-p3 =Until then, a USB Wi-Fi stick works on any computer with no setup. Plug it in and it shows in Network. Nothing is changed on your computer by this message.

## Topic: Playing games (src/settings/help_topics.rs)

# Title of the Help topic "Playing games".
settings-help-topics-games-title = Playing games
# Words people might search for the Help topic "Playing games" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-games-keywords = games game steam proton windows launcher ubisoft controller gamepad anti-cheat vanguard black screen will not start crash alt-tab hdr
# Help topic "Playing games": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-games-p1 = Steam works. Install it from Apps. It runs as a Flatpak, in a box of its own, and plays Windows games through Proton. Ubisoft Connect was checked. Other launchers usually work inside Steam, but each can break after its own update. Games made for Linux, DirectX 12 games and older 32-bit games have not been checked yet.
# Help topic "Playing games": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-games-p2 = Trindle raises the memory limit and the file limit that big games need. A game that holds the mouse keeps it, and lets it go when you press Alt-Tab. A full-screen game stays full screen behind the window you switched to. The screen stays awake while a full-screen game is in front.
# Help topic "Playing games": paragraph 3, before the numbered steps.
settings-help-topics-games-p3 = When a game will not start, try these in order.
# Help topic "Playing games": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-games-step4-1 = Restart Steam. Close it fully and open it again. A Steam that has run for days can stop games starting.
# Help topic "Playing games": step 2 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-games-step4-2 = Wait. The first start of a game builds its shaders, and the screen can stay black for a few minutes.
# Help topic "Playing games": step 3 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-games-step4-3 = Check the Proton version in the game's Properties, under Compatibility, and try another.
# Help topic "Playing games": step 4 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-games-step4-4 = Sign in to the game's own launcher, such as Ubisoft Connect. Its window can hide behind the full-screen game. Press Alt-Tab to find it.
# Help topic "Playing games": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-games-p5 = Anti-cheat that runs inside the kernel, such as Vanguard, cannot run on Linux at all. Easy Anti-Cheat and BattlEye work only for a game whose publisher has allowed Linux.
# Help topic "Playing games": paragraph 6.
settings-help-topics-games-p6 = The keyboard and mouse work. PlayStation, Switch and many Xbox controllers, and Steam Input, may not work in games yet. Trindle has a small service for game controllers, but the parts that use it are not finished.
# Help topic "Playing games": tip 7.
settings-help-topics-games-tip7 = HDR and variable refresh rate are not supported yet.
